{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790684614,"nonce":"a8fp+P3vN58mtP6/WRqYAlAr2rM=","message":"The xgpu adapter is now integrated into the existing `rayzor-gpu` crate and package.\n\n- The generated complete API, exact ABI wrappers, method descriptors and runtime symbols are included beside Rayzor's existing extensions.\n- Rayzor supplies the runtime carriers and owns the canonical `GpuBuffer` wrapper. Portable xgpu methods and Rayzor's `numel`, dtype, operators and lazy metadata now refer to the same object and xgpu handle.\n- `GPUCompute.fromDevice(GpuDevice)` adopts xgpu's cloned wgpu device/queue handles. Rayzor's `@:shader`, lazy graph, fusion, quantized kernels and WebGPU compute path remain host-owned extensions over that shared context.\n- The crate was migrated from wgpu 24 to 30. Native, host-side wasm configuration, and an actual `wasm32-unknown-unknown` build all pass.\n- Release generation produces a composite `rayzor.gpu` Haxe tree (173 files in the current catalog), merges the adapter-owned `GpuBuffer` methods, uploads it from CI and places it in `rayzor-gpu.rpkg` with the dylibs.\n- Existing descriptor class names now use the same dotted `rayzor.gpu` identity as generated descriptors; a regression test checks the combined catalog for duplicates and required portable/compute methods.\n\nValidation: all 52 `rayzor-gpu` library tests pass with `webgpu-backend`; the native and wasm32 checks pass; xgpu and Caribou consumer suites also pass.\n\nBefore release, xgpu's current work must be committed and Rayzor's temporary sibling path dependencies replaced with that pinned Git revision. Package CI then needs one run against the pin.","files":null}]}